Chapter: I219K1Mechanism
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 THIS ARM ASKS WHETHER TRANSMIT TRACKS THE CITED CONTROL, which is a narrower
 question than whether the driver works and is why it is separate from
 `e1000-pch-k1`. That arm asks whether the step lifts the stall. This one ties
 each transmit to the VALUE of the register beside it, so a green cannot be
 read off a step that ran without the bits landing.

 The control is I219 rev 2.02 section 9.5.5.2: PHY address 01, page 770,
 register 17, bit 13 `Giga_K1_disable` and bit 14 `K1 enable`, both RW and
 both defaulting to 0b. 16384 is bit 14 alone, the model's NVM power-up value
 under `-i219-k1-nvm 1`; 24576 is bits 14 and 13 together, which is what
 disabling K1 at gigabit leaves behind.

 THE ARM PRODUCES ITS OWN LEADING KILL AND MUST KEEP DOING SO. With
 `e1000-pch-k1-required` shipping True, `e1000-init` configures K1 before this
 arm reads anything, so the shipped state is a PASS and a ladder that started
 there would read pass, pass, kill, pass -- one kill, at the end, after two
 passes have already been banked. The `e1000-phy-bring-up` ahead of row 1 is
 what prevents that: section 5.2 says a PHY soft reset returns the PHY
 registers to their defaults while leaving other registers alone, so 770.17
 goes back to its NVM value and the part is stalled again. Every kill in this
 ladder is manufactured by that reset, which is also the post-reset obligation
 the same section states: "the integrated LAN controller configures the LCD
 registers".

 The ladder is kill, pass, kill, pass, and each kill costs one reset. Do not
 "simplify" it by reading the state `e1000-init` leaves: that row is a pass and
 it belongs at the END, where it cannot displace the leading kill.

Section: Fixtures

  i219-pm-page : Integer = 770
  i219-pm-reg : Integer = 17

  bring-up : PciScanResult -> E1000Device
  bring-up (scan) =
    when e1000-find scan
      is Just (d) -> e1000-init d
      is None -> e1000-absent

  mk-frame : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  mk-frame (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc else mk-frame (i + 1) n (list-push acc i)

Section: Judges

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  pm-read : Integer -> Integer
  pm-read (mmio) =
   let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio i219-pm-page
   in let v = e1000-phy-read mmio i219-pm-reg
   in let b = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
   in v

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let d = bring-up (pci-scan-bus 0)
    in let mmio = d.e-mmio
    in let at-init = pm-read mmio
    in let wipe1 = e1000-phy-bring-up mmio
    in let cited-killed = pm-read mmio
    in let send-killed = e1000-send-frame d (mk-frame 0 64 [])
    in let step1 = e1000-k1-configure mmio False
    in let cited-stepped = pm-read mmio
    in let send-stepped = e1000-send-frame d (mk-frame 0 64 [])
    in let wipe2 = e1000-phy-bring-up mmio
    in let cited-killed2 = pm-read mmio
    in let send-killed2 = e1000-send-frame d (mk-frame 0 64 [])
    in let step2 = e1000-k1-configure mmio False
    in let cited-again = pm-read mmio
    in let send-again = e1000-send-frame d (mk-frame 0 64 [])
    in act
      print-line-uni ("present            : " & yn (d.e-present))
      print-line-uni ("cited after wipe   : " & show cited-killed)
      print-line-uni ("send after wipe    : " & show send-killed)
      print-line-uni ("step answers       : " & show step1)
      print-line-uni ("cited after step   : " & show cited-stepped)
      print-line-uni ("send after step    : " & show send-stepped)
      print-line-uni ("cited after wipe 2 : " & show cited-killed2)
      print-line-uni ("send after wipe 2  : " & show send-killed2)
      print-line-uni ("cited after step 2 : " & show cited-again)
      print-line-uni ("send after step 2  : " & show send-again)
      print-line-uni ("cited left by init : " & show at-init)
    end
  end

